<section class="mr-section"><h2>A reason for every later diagonal turn</h2><p>This is a modern specialization of the condition in X.2. Write k = √2 − 1, so 0 &lt; k &lt; 1. The first subtraction leaves k and produces the pair (1, k). Exactly two k fit into 1, because 1/k = 1 + √2 is strictly between 2 and 3. The remainder is 1 − 2k = 3 − 2√2 = k² &gt; 0.</p>${z(I)}<p>The new pair (k, k²) equals k × (1, k). Multiplying both lengths by the same positive number preserves the copy count and gives a positive scaled remainder. The same argument applies again after any finite number of turns: zero is never reached. The finite experiment is limited to twenty-four turns; this identity supplies the infinite condition.</p><p>If a positive length E measured both originals, it would also measure every remainder, because a whole multiple can be subtracted without losing that property. Every nonzero grouped remainder is less than half the longer input: if B ≤ A/2, then R &lt; B ≤ A/2; otherwise q = 1 and R = A − B &lt; A/2. Repeated turns eventually leave a positive remainder smaller than E, which cannot contain even one E. X.2’s contradiction therefore rules out the supposed common length measure.</p><h3>Yet the squares share an area measure</h3>${z(P)}<p>The squares on the side and diagonal have areas one and two in side-square units. They are commensurable in square, in the sense of Book X’s second definition. This does not supply a common measure of the two lengths.</p></section>
